# AMD64 thunk export spec -- cfgmgr32.dll.
#
# Steam's own GameOverlayRenderer64.dll imports this module statically, and it
# was the ONE module of the ten it imports that had no guest thunk at all.
# [MEASURED] 2026-08-18, ppc64le/thunks/import_chain.py on
# ~/.local/share/Steam/GameOverlayRenderer64.dll: kernel32, user32, gdi32,
# advapi32, ole32, oleaut32, winmm, imm32 and psapi all had one; cfgmgr32 did
# not.  On this port that difference is the difference between the two failure
# shapes ppc64le/games/STATUS.md opens with -- a missing EXPORT binds a
# sentinel and dies by name at the first call, a missing MODULE fails the whole
# import walk before a single guest instruction runs.
#
# It is also ordinary furniture rather than anything to do with Steam: cfgmgr32
# is the Configuration Manager, and any guest DLL that enumerates devices --
# an input library, an audio library, a monitor-topology query -- imports it.
# Boltgun's and Styx's fifteen were the same discovery about the same class.
#
# Most of this module's 276 spec entries are `stub`, which spec2thunk does not
# emit for on any module; what is emitted is the implemented surface.  Its
# declarations live in cfgmgr32.h, which windows.h does not pull in, so the
# header is named here for the same reason dwmapi.h and winldap.h are named in
# theirs: without it the declaration does not exist and every export it
# declares is refused with the compiler's own reason.
DLL cfgmgr32.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

PROBE-EXTRA cfgmgr32.h
